        <span class="hero-tag">01 / Introduction</span>
        <h1>A Crisis That Demanded<br /><em>an Industry</em></h1>
        <p>
          Two decades of rising youth mental health struggles, a smartphone revolution,
          and the educators who refused to look away — this is how Social-Emotional
          Learning became a global imperative.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>
 
    <main class="container">
 
      <!-- ABSTRACT -->
      <section class="fade-in">
        <p class="lead-text">
          As the global education sector grapples with the rising mental health crisis
          in young students, Social-Emotional Learning has evolved from supplemental
          education into a multi-billion dollar technology-driven industry.
        </p>
        <p>
          This ePublication provides an in-depth analysis of the Social-Emotional Learning
          industry, exploring the transition from traditional classroom instruction to a
          dynamic ecosystem where mental health, physical movement, and data analysis
          intersect. From the foundational Collaborative for Academic, Social, and Emotional
          Learning (CASEL) frameworks to the rise of Wellbeing-as-a-Service, this ePublication
          examines emerging trends for the future of Social-Emotional Learning.
        </p>
      </section>
 
      <!-- CRISIS SECTION -->
      <div class="subsection-title fade-in">
        <h3>Youth Mental Health: A Nation Reckoning with Its Children</h3>
      </div>
 
      <div class="crisis-layout fade-in">
        <div class="crisis-text">
          <p>
            For more than two decades, a growing number of US youth have been presenting to
            emergency rooms for mental health issues. The numbers tell a stark story — but
            behind every statistic is a student, a classroom, and a community struggling
            to respond.
          </p>
          <p>
            Suicide rates among young people increased <strong>85.3% between 2007 and 2017</strong>.
            More than 1 in 5 high school students reported seriously considering suicide in
            the previous year. 9.5% attempted suicide, and 2.3% made attempts requiring
            medical care.
          </p>
          <p>
            These were not isolated incidents — they were the symptom of a systemic failure
            to address the social and emotional needs of young people alongside their
            academic development.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="crisis-stats stagger-parent">
          <div class="crisis-stat-card">
            <div class="cs-value">85.3%</div>
            <div class="cs-desc">Rise in youth suicide rates between 2007–2017</div>
          </div>
          <div class="crisis-stat-card">
            <div class="cs-value">1 in 5</div>
            <div class="cs-desc">High school students seriously considered suicide in the past year</div>
          </div>
          <div class="crisis-stat-card">
            <div class="cs-value">9.5%</div>
            <div class="cs-desc">Of high school students attempted suicide</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
 
      <!-- SMARTPHONE SECTION -->
      <div class="subsection-title fade-in">
        <h3>The Smartphone Generation</h3>
      </div>
 
      <div class="smartphone-section fade-in">
        <p>
          With smartphones landing in the hands of younger generations, educators saw
          dramatic changes in how students communicate, treat others, how isolated they
          feel, and how they perceive themselves.
        </p>
        <p>
          According to a national survey carried out by EdWeek Research Center, 595 teachers,
          school leaders, and district leaders felt concerned about K-12 students being
          constantly online. Social media introduced new vectors for social comparison,
          cyberbullying, and a distorted sense of self-worth that traditional SEL
          frameworks were not designed to address.
        </p>
 
        <div class="impact-grid stagger-parent">
          <div class="impact-card">
            <div class="impact-icon">📱</div>
            <h4>Always Connected</h4>
            <p>Constant connectivity blurred the line between school and social life, making emotional regulation harder for developing brains.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="impact-card">
            <div class="impact-icon">🔇</div>
            <h4>Rising Isolation</h4>
            <p>Despite being more "connected" than any generation before, rates of loneliness and social disconnection climbed steadily.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="impact-card">
            <div class="impact-icon">🔄</div>
            <h4>The SEL Response</h4>
            <p>Rather than removing technology, educators worked toward helping students use social media in ways that support mental health and wellbeing.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
 
      <!-- ABOUT / AUTHOR -->
      <div class="subsection-title fade-in">
        <h3>About This ePublication</h3>
      </div>
 
      <div class="author-section fade-in">
        <div class="author-card">
          <div class="author-avatar">KP</div>
          <div class="author-info">
            <p class="author-name">Kiannah Pierce</p>
            <p class="author-role">Innovative Specialist · MEITE Program · University of North Carolina · 2026</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p>
          This ePublication centers on a business profile of GoNoodle, a high-performing
          industry leader that has bridged the gap between physical activity and mental
          regulation for over 40 million children. The analysis examines GoNoodle's
          business model, innovative features such as its classroom-to-home connectivity,
          as well as key weaknesses and critical areas for growth.
        </p>
        <p>
          It also examines key competitors and industry players for student
          social-emotional wellbeing, and explores the unique connection between
          activity and Social-Emotional Learning. This ePublication concludes with a
          roadmap for stakeholders to implement evidence-based tools that democratize
          mental health support and help drive long-term student success across
          diverse backgrounds — key focuses of GoNoodle's mission.
        </p>
 
